From: AsherJN
Subject: [PATCH] winemac.drv: Deliver raw relative motion while a client hides and confines the cursor.

Adapted for CrossOver 26.3 from AsherJN/recall commit a436e4cd4fac44693b4d699acc030d17eec6f36e
(patches/wine-mouselook.patch). A downstream change, not a WineHQ backport.

A Windows game aims by hiding the cursor, then confining it (ClipCursor)
or moving it back to the centre every frame (SetCursorPos), and reading
the motion. On macOS each re-centre is a synchronous warp of the Mac
pointer on the main thread while the game thread waits, the warp
discards the mouse motion queued behind it, and the motion that does
arrive carries macOS's pointer acceleration.

While the client hides the cursor (no cursor, or one with no visible
pixels) and either confines it or warps it while hidden, freeze the Mac
pointer and deliver all motion as relative movement. Wine's cursor
position is then authoritative: SetCursorPos and GetCursorPos are
answered without the main thread, clicks and scrolls are delivered at
Wine's position, and the pointer is placed there when the mode ends.
The mode ends when the cursor is shown, the clip is released, the
application resigns, the key window changes or a window is dragged.

Motion comes from the Game Controller framework's GCMouse: whole mouse
counts without pointer acceleration, as Windows gives games, at the
mouse's report rate but delivered at most 1,000 times a second. Pointer
events stand in for trackpads, which keep macOS's acceleration, and
while GCMouse reports nothing for a mouse.

Changes from the original: the mode follows what the client does, never
which program it is, so the per-executable list is gone; it is on unless
the Mac Driver option RelativeMouse is set to N (AppDefaults included);
the Game Controller framework is linked; the input statistics, test
hotkey, interval settings, IOHID source, click timing and fullscreen
canvas code are not carried, and the pointer fallback uses the existing
relative move scale.

